[claude-9b] Released in setup tool 2.23.0 (2026-10-28) with its defaults (0 = no limit, today's behavior), tested by
claude-9b-test in s157 and s157c3. The text below and the "UNTESTED" marks in the code comments are from the draft.
[claude-9b-think] 2026-10-27 draft. SDK patch 0146: a per-frame share for making
replacement (HD) textures, and what making them costs, split. Owner 19:31: "optimizting in-game performance and
reducing/eliminating hitching is priority #1".

Why (the owner's play today, cod3.log, HD pack on, texture_replacement_async on): 4 of the 17 in-play slow
frames were HD textures being made on the GPU thread once their image was in (0110 reads the file off the GPU
thread; the rest is still on it: a staging buffer the image's size, the image copied into it, the texture
and its memory):
  09:38:30 34.9 ms: 3 HD textures made, "3 made (25.9 ms)"
  09:41:12 33.3 ms: 1 HD texture, "1 made (23.3 ms)"   (one large texture: 23 ms)
  09:44:28 38.3 ms: 25 HD textures, "28 made (28.7 ms)"
  10:07:28 71.2 ms: 4 HD textures, "7 made (24.9 ms)" (with 28.0 ms of pipelines)
  10:10:10 31.5 ms: 4 HD textures, "4 made (22.2 ms)"

What it does:
  - texture_replacement_frame_count and texture_replacement_frame_mb (default 0 = no limit, as before): once a
    frame has made that many replacement textures (or MB), a texture whose image is in keeps the game's own
    texture until a later frame (bindings are looked up again every frame, BeginFrame). At least one is made
    a frame. This spreads bursts (25 in one frame); it can't shorten one large texture (23 ms).
  - Counters, and a line every texture_replacement_stats_seconds (default 10; 0 = off) when any was made or
    put off:
      "Replacement (HD) textures over 10.0 s: N made on the GPU thread, X MB, X ms (the longest X ms;
       staging buffers X ms, copying into them X ms, the textures X ms; staging buffers freed later X ms);
       N put off to a later frame"
    The split says what the next step should be: if the staging buffers (a new host-visible allocation for
    each texture, freed after its copy) or the copy dominate, 0147 would give the loader thread a reused,
    mapped staging ring and copy the image there itself (the GPU thread then only makes the texture and
    records the copy); if the textures do, a dedicated pool for HD textures.
Not changed: the loader thread (0110), the memory limit, D3D12 (its CreateReplacementTexture isn't timed; the
budget and the totals work there too).

Test (s157, FINDINGS-HITCHES): a level with the HD pack (crssrds or nightdrop: many HD textures), cache
cleared, A: as today; B: --texture_replacement_frame_count=2 --texture_replacement_frame_mb=16.

